/* =========================================================
   PAZIENTE_BANK — «Come parlano i pazienti»: how real people
   describe symptoms. [phrase, meaning, plausible-wrong, note]
========================================================= */
const PAZIENTE_BANK = [
["Mi scoppia la testa","splitting headache","my head itches","Scoppiare = to burst. The everyday word for severe headache — nobody says «cefalea» at home."],
["Mi gira la testa","I feel dizzy","I keep changing my mind","The standard vertigo/dizziness phrase: the head 'spins'."],
["Ho il fiatone","I'm short of breath (on exertion)","I have bad breath","Fiato = breath; fiatone = the big panting breath after stairs. Everyday dyspnea."],
["Mi manca il respiro","I can't breathe properly","I forgot to breathe","More alarming than fiatone — air hunger at rest. Triage ears up."],
["Ho un peso sullo stomaco","heaviness/indigestion after eating","I gained belly weight","Classic dyspepsia description: a 'weight' sitting on the stomach."],
["Mi sento la febbre addosso","I feel feverish","I'm scared of fevers","Addosso = on me. The chills-and-aches feeling before the thermometer confirms."],
["Ho la nausea e mi viene da vomitare","I feel sick and about to vomit","I hate vomiting","«Mi viene da…» = I'm on the verge of. Imminent, not hypothetical."],
["Mi fa male la bocca dello stomaco","epigastric pain","my mouth tastes bad","La bocca dello stomaco = the epigastrium. Beloved of nonne everywhere — and of cardiologists, who hear 'rule out MI'."],
["Ho le gambe che non mi reggono","my legs feel weak / give way","my trousers don't fit","Reggere = to hold up. Weakness or presyncope, not a joke about jeans."],
["Mi sono sentita mancare","I nearly fainted","I felt unwanted","Sentirsi mancare = presyncope. Follow up: did she actually lose consciousness?"],
["Ho un dolore che mi prende qui e va al braccio","pain here radiating to the arm","my arm fell asleep","Radiating pain, patient-style. If 'here' is the chest: move fast."],
["Mi sento il cuore in gola","my heart is racing/pounding","I have a lump in my throat from emotion","Palpitations — the heart 'in the throat'. Context separates it from anxiety-idiom use."],
["Ho lo stomaco sottosopra","upset stomach","I rearranged my kitchen","Sottosopra = upside down. General GI upset."],
["Non ho chiuso occhio","I didn't sleep at all","I kept my eyes open on purpose","Insomnia idiom: 'I didn't close an eye'."],
["Sono sempre stanco morto","I'm exhausted all the time","I think about death often","Stanco morto = dead tired. Chronic fatigue flag, not psychiatry per se."],
["Mi formicola la mano","my hand is tingling","my hand smells of ants","Formicolare, from formica (ant): paresthesia — ants crawling."],
["Ho le vampate di calore","hot flushes","I love spicy food","Vampata = a flare of heat. Menopause consult vocabulary."],
["Mi brucia quando faccio pipì","burning on urination","I'm embarrassed to urinate","Bruciore + pipì: the UTI opener, said plainly."],
["Vado di corpo ogni tre giorni","I move my bowels every three days","I exercise every three days","Andare di corpo = the polite bowel euphemism. Learn it or miss the constipation entirely."],
["Ho la pancia gonfia","my belly is bloated","I'm pregnant","Gonfio = swollen. Bloating — though in context, always clarify."],
["Mi è venuto il torcicollo","I've got a stiff neck","my neck is twisted permanently","Torci-collo = twist-neck. Woke up unable to turn the head."],
["Ho preso un colpo d'aria","I caught a chill (draft)","I was hit by the wind","The great Italian folk illness: drafts cause everything. Nod, then examine."],
["Ho la cervicale","my neck aches (chronic)","I have a cervix problem","«La cervicale» = neck pain as a national institution. Not gynecology."],
["Mi sento uno sfarfallio al petto","fluttering in my chest (palpitations)","butterflies from being in love","Sfarfallio, from farfalla (butterfly): arrhythmia-speak in the wild."],
["Ho il sangue dal naso","my nose is bleeding","I have high blood pressure","Epistaxis, plainly. Follow up on frequency and meds."],
["Mi tira la schiena","my back is tight/pulling","someone is pulling my jacket","Tirare = to pull: muscle tightness or lumbago, the builder's classic."]
];
